<article>
<header>
<h1>Evergreen Capability Loop</h1>
<p>Turn model-assisted work into portable capabilities and independently inspectable evidence. The scarce resources are reliable feedback, human attention and maintenance capacity, not the number of generated files.</p>
<p>This is an application layer over the existing repository-autocomplete toolkit. It does not change RAPP/1, run an unlimited model service, or promote experiments into a stable runtime.</p>
</header>
<section id="components">
<h2>1. The three working components</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>scripts/capability_registry.py</code></td><td>Validate job manifests and source pins, resolve dependency relationships, and derive built/proven/reused status from qualified, RAPP-bound uses rather than editable self-ratings.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>scripts/capability_package.py</code></td><td>Create deterministic source capsules from exact Git commits, restore them without overlaying user data, and qualify/replay the declared operation with real commands and source-byte checks.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>scripts/capability_scheduler.py</code></td><td>Rank source/model/failure-triggered work, enforce an explicit capability allowlist and finite policy, execute supported jobs in separate attempt directories, preserve outcomes, and refuse duplicate or ambiguous execution.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The initial executable job is deliberately narrow: transfer and safely restore exact public UTF-8 source from a committed repository. A source capsule is not a claim that the packaged application has all of its runtime dependencies or that an arbitrary generated program is correct.</p>
</section>
<section id="asset">
<h2>2. What counts as a capability</h2>
<p>A capability manifest binds a stable ID/version to a concrete job, a byte-pinned entrypoint and dependencies, input/output descriptions, explicit permissions, bounded nonempty checks, reproducible failure cases, reuse relationships and a public/private decision.</p>
<p>The manifest's raw SHA-256 is its revision. Changing code invalidates the old pin and the old qualification for that new revision. No asset becomes reusable just because its title sounds useful.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Required evidence</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Built</td><td>The validated manifest and its exact implementation bytes exist.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proven</td><td>At least one matching qualification was replayed by a real successful command, with the report, capsule, manifest and implementation bound into a verified RAPP record.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reused</td><td>The same capability revision completed qualifying jobs in at least two distinct repository/commit/tree contexts. Repeating one job or renaming a checkout does not count twice.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>There is no automatic promotion to shared default, Core or Grail. Measured reuse makes an asset a candidate for that separate owner-controlled release decision. Preserve experimental Frontier isolation and existing release boundaries.</p>
<p>The input/output schemas describe the contract; native CLI validation enforces the implemented operation. Permission declarations are admission rules, not an operating-system sandbox. Manifest commands remain trusted local processes.</p>
</section>
<section id="cycle">
<h2>3. A bounded cycle</h2>
<ol>
<li>Read the current capability registry, source baseline, policy, known failures and open work. Search by job before creating a replacement.</li>
<li>Pin the actual source commit and capability manifest revision. A model-upgrade event is a reason to requalify existing assets before generating new ones.</li>
<li>Build a plan from explicit requests. The policy allows exact capability revisions, permissions, concurrency, attempts, time and visibility. Missing authority or evidence is blocked work, not an invitation to guess.</li>
<li>Execute only supported, admitted jobs. The initial scheduler runs source-capsule qualification; an unsupported job becomes a host work order rather than a pretend success.</li>
<li>Verify the capsule against committed source, restore it into a fresh destination, run the manifest's real checks, and ensure the declared implementation did not change during the operation.</li>
<li>Use the existing RAPP recorder to bind the stable report/capsule/manifest/source objects while actually executing the report's replay command. Each job has its own memory stream.</li>
<li>Rebuild the registry from verified records. Retain failures and stale-version evidence without graduating them. Repeated execution of an already completed idempotency key must be a no-op.</li>
<li>Use the new evidence to choose the next bounded objective. Stop on exhausted budgets, missing permissions, stale locks, ambiguous recovery or lack of a credible useful task.</li>
</ol>
<p>Every accepted change must either add a useful capability or make an existing useful operation more reliable. Increasing the number of frames, manifests, reports or prompts is not an improvement metric.</p>
</section>
<section id="commands">
<h2>4. Commands and inputs</h2>
<p>Run commands from the capability repository root. The RAPP reference must match the existing <code>landgrab/autocomplete/rapp-reference.json</code>. The Python tools use the standard library; executed qualification checks require the existing POSIX process controls.</p>
<pre><code>python3 scripts/capability_registry.py build --help
python3 scripts/capability_registry.py search --help
python3 scripts/capability_package.py qualify --help
python3 scripts/capability_package.py verify --help
python3 scripts/capability_scheduler.py plan --help
python3 scripts/capability_scheduler.py run --help</code></pre>
<p>Manifests live under <code>landgrab/autocomplete/capabilities/manifests/</code>. A request identifies the capability, source checkout, exact source commit, public repository name, selected source paths, workflow, trigger and requested model. A policy admits exact manifest hashes and limits jobs, parallelism, attempts, command time and visibility.</p>
<p>Planning does not execute code. Running an approved plan is explicit. An empty eligible queue is idle, not a quota that must be filled. Source paths and restored files are validated before writes; existing destinations and dangerous path aliases are refused.</p>
<p>Global RAPP verification and registry publication happen after writers are quiescent. Do not run a verifier of the same live store as a command inside an active record append: the append lock correctly marks that store busy.</p>
</section>
<section id="models">
<h2>5. How future models continue the work</h2>
<p>A model is a replaceable worker, not the capability's identity. Its first task is to read the registry and reproduce the existing hard cases. It should reuse proven components, investigate actual failures, and create a narrowly scoped patch only when the current contracts cannot complete a useful job.</p>
<p>Keep model selection and integration branch in explicit policy rather than hard-coding an old provider or assuming every repository uses the same branch name. A <code>requested_model</code> or model-upgrade trigger is not evidence that that model was invoked. The deterministic scheduler must report <code>model_execution: not_invoked</code> for its own qualification work; an external coding host separately records actual model execution when claiming it.</p>
<p>Independent criticism, negative controls and actual source/restore outcomes are required. Multiple copies of the same frontier model agreeing do not create ground truth. Frozen evaluations must not be weakened by the builder to manufacture progress.</p>
<p>Useful next work includes better failure cases, safer connectors, more portable operations, and genuinely successful second uses. First-party or licensed data may improve those assets, but synthetic feedback is not evidence of real demand.</p>
</section>
<section id="reuse">
<h2>6. The first reuse milestone</h2>
<p>The demonstration applies one unchanged source-capsule implementation to two actual public projects: <code>kody-w/localFirstTools</code> and <code>kody-w/openrappter</code>. Each context names an exact source commit and its selected public files; the Apache-2.0 license and notice accompany the OpenRappter component.</p>
<p>The measured reuse claim is configuration-only second use: identical capability manifest and implementation hashes, a different repository/commit/tree, a different workflow, independently restored exact bytes, and new replay evidence. It is not a claim of human adoption, financial savings, general application execution, or cross-model intelligence.</p>
<p>Failure cases include changed source pins, malformed reports, unsafe restoration paths, occupied destinations, failed commands, duplicate jobs, policy drift and stale execution state. These cases remain assets for the next frontier model to inherit.</p>
</section>
<section id="boundaries">
<h2>7. What this does not authorize</h2>
<ul>
<li>No automatic model calls, production deployment, branch merge, external message, purchase or public release follows merely from a plan or frame.</li>
<li>Private examples, credentials and personal data stay out of public capsules and evidence. The initial demonstration uses only selected public source.</li>
<li>A local timestamp and unsigned RAPP history are not authenticated authorship, independent time attestation or proof of first invention.</li>
<li>A successful source capsule is not a claim that a whole application compiles or that its business behavior is correct. The receipt names the exact job and checks that ran.</li>
<li>An interrupted job is not automatically retried when side effects are ambiguous. Preserve the attempt and require explicit safe recovery.</li>
<li>Preserve all evidence, but promote only qualified implementations. Do not merge broken code simply to preserve a worker's contribution.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="handoff">
<h2>8. Operator startup prompt</h2>
<pre><code>Read the approved capability registry and policy before creating new software.
Pin the source repository and capability revision for one valuable job.
Search for reusable operations and examine their failure cases first.
Use the scheduler's bounded plan; blocked work is not permission to bypass a gate.
Run admitted qualifications and retain actual replay/RAPP evidence.
For unsupported work, use isolated coding worktrees and an explicit host work order.
Never equate requested model metadata with actual model execution.
Graduation requires successful independent reuse, not a self-assigned status.
Publish only through the approved review path and preserve the prior history.
Leave the next useful bounded action and its evidence for the next model.</code></pre>
</section>
